WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:09.680
Genetic Slavery Your genes trying to kill you with nuclear weapons and preventable disease simultaneously, which is an impressive

2
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.480
amount of multitasking for software that was originally designed to find berries.

3
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.940
How Hydrogen Learned to Worry (A thirteen point eight billion year recap).

4
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:26.040
Thirteen point eight billion years ago, the universe exploded.

5
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.380
This was the last time anything interesting happened for about four hundred million years.

6
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:38.740
Then hydrogen atoms, which had been drifting around doing nothing (hydrogen is the simplest element because the universe

7
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:45.200
started with low expectations), began clumping together until they got so dense they caught fire.

8
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:53.660
These fires are called "stars." Stars are the universe's first manufacturing process: they take hydrogen, crush it into

9
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:59.920
heavier elements like carbon and oxygen, and then explode, scattering their products across space.

10
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:07.120
The workplace fatality rate is one hundred percent. This went on for about nine billion years.

11
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:16.320
Then, on one unremarkable rock orbiting one unremarkable star in one unremarkable galaxy, carbon atoms started doing something

12
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:23.560
unusual. Carbon is the most promiscuous element in the periodic table; it will bond with almost anything.

13
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:32.940
On your particular rock, it bonded with hydrogen, oxygen, and nitrogen in arrangements that could do one very specific trick:

14
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.760
copy themselves. This was the moment everything went wrong.

15
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.020
The molecules that were better at copying made more copies.

16
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:49.340
The ones that were worse at copying made fewer. This is the entire plot of the next four billion years.

17
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:57.000
It is also the entire plot of your economy, your politics, your wars, and your inability to fund clinical trials.

18
00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:06.500
Everything else is a footnote. Including you. Four billion years of copying molecules competing with other copying molecules

19
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:14.960
eventually produced a copying molecule so complicated it could look up at the stars that made it and ask, "Why am I anxious

20
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:23.060
about a meeting tomorrow?" The answer is: because anxiety made your ancestors copy more effectively than calm did.

21
00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:30.680
You are a temporary vehicle that copying molecules built to make more copying molecules, and the vehicle has become sentient

22
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:37.520
enough to realize this, which is either the most beautiful or the most horrifying thing in the universe depending on how

23
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:44.080
you feel about being a meat taxi for chemistry. But the vehicle wasn't built all at once.

24
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.120
It was built in layers, like a bad renovation where nobody removed the previous tenants' plumbing.

25
00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:58.300
First came the reptilian brain: the oldest layer, roughly five hundred million years old, handling the functions so basic

26
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:04.260
they're barely worth listing (breathe, eat, fight, flee, reproduce, regulate body temperature).

27
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.340
This is the brain you share with lizards. It has no feelings.

28
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:16.240
It has no opinions. It has reflexes, and those reflexes kept vertebrates alive for three hundred million years before anything

29
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.380
with fur showed up. It's still running. It's running right now.

30
00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.780
It's the reason your heart is beating without your permission.

31
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:31.160
Then, about two hundred million years ago, mammals evolved the limbic system on top of the reptile brain, like building

32
00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.640
a nursery on top of a weapons depot. This is your emotional brain.

33
00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:41.560
It's the reason you bond with your children instead of eating them (a genuine upgrade over the reptilian model).

34
00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:50.100
It handles fear, pleasure, memory, and social bonding. It's the reason you cry at movies, love your dog, and feel a physical

35
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:57.420
ache when someone you care about is hurting. It is also the reason you make catastrophically stupid decisions when you're

36
00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:04.040
angry, aroused, or afraid, because the limbic system processes information faster than the part of your brain that thinks,

37
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:10.320
and by the time thinking arrives, the limbic system has already committed you to a course of action you'll spend the next

38
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:18.339
decade regretting. Finally, very recently in evolutionary terms (roughly two to three million years ago, which is yesterday

39
00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:22.760
by the universe's standards), the neocortex expanded dramatically.

40
00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:30.840
This is the part you think of as "you." It handles language, abstract reasoning, planning, and the ability to contemplate

41
00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.740
your own mortality and then do absolutely nothing about it.

42
00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:38.600
It's the thinnest layer, the newest addition, and the most easily overridden.

43
00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:46.100
Your neocortex can calculate orbital mechanics, prove mathematical theorems, and design nuclear reactors.

44
00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:52.680
Your limbic system can shut all of that down with a single flush of cortisol because someone looked at you funny.

45
00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:58.740
Your reptilian brain can override both of them simultaneously because it heard a loud noise.

46
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.460
You are three brains in a trench coat pretending to be one person.

47
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.080
The reptile wants to survive. The mammal wants to be loved.

48
00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:15.630
The human wants to understand the universe. They take turns driving, none of them have a license, and the reptile has seniority.

49
00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.540
The chemicals don't know you exist. They have no plan. They can't plan.

50
00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:30.080
They're chemicals. But through thirteen point eight billion years of mindless repetition, they produced a species with three

51
00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:38.280
stacked brains that can split atoms, write symphonies, and cure diseases, but mostly uses these abilities to argue on the

52
00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.360
internet and build weapons. If you're looking for someone to blame, there's nobody.

53
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.440
There's just hydrogen, which caught fire and then got very, very out of hand.

54
00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.500
The Selfish Gene Made You Illogical (It Was a Good Idea at the Time).

55
00:05:53.100 --> 00:06:00.940
For about a century, your biologists were stuck on a puzzle that, from the outside, was hilarious to watch.

56
00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:07.940
Darwin said natural selection acts on individuals: the fittest survive, the weakest don't, end of story.

57
00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:17.240
Elegant, simple, and completely unable to explain why a ground squirrel would scream to warn its colony about a hawk, thereby

58
00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:24.640
making itself the most obvious target. Individual selection says that squirrel should shut up and let everyone else get

59
00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:32.400
eaten. The screamer dies. The quiet ones survive. Within a few generations, screaming should be extinct.

60
00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:40.530
But it isn't. The squirrels keep screaming. Darwin's theory, applied to individuals, predicts a world of pure selfishness.

61
00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:46.040
Your world has firefighters, organ donors, and people who jump on grenades.

62
00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:55.380
Something didn't add up. So some of your biologists tried group selection: maybe evolution acts on whole groups.

63
00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.340
Altruistic groups outcompete selfish groups, so altruism survives.

64
00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:06.910
This sounded lovely and was almost entirely wrong. The problem is cheaters.

65
00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:14.620
Drop one selfish individual into an altruistic group, and that individual gets all the benefits of everyone else's sacrifice

66
00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:21.080
while paying none of the costs. The selfish gene spreads. The altruistic ones disappear.

67
00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.220
Within a few generations, the group is full of cheaters again.

68
00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:33.740
Group selection is the evolutionary equivalent of communism: beautiful on paper, immediately destroyed by the first person

69
00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:41.280
who realizes they can get away with not contributing. Your biologists spent decades arguing about this.

70
00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:48.200
On Wishonia, we found the debate very entertaining, in the way you find it entertaining when someone can't find the glasses

71
00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:55.520
they're already wearing. The answer, when it finally arrived, was so simple it was almost insulting.

72
00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:02.950
Hamilton and then Dawkins pointed out that neither the individual nor the group is the unit of selection.

73
00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.560
The gene is. Genes don't care about the organism they're sitting in.

74
00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:12.120
They care about copies of themselves, wherever those copies happen to be.

75
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.660
That screaming ground squirrel isn't sacrificing itself for the group.

76
00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:23.850
It's sacrificing itself for its relatives, who carry copies of the same genes, including the gene for screaming.

77
00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:32.740
The math works: if the scream saves enough copies of the screaming gene in nearby relatives, the gene spreads, even though

78
00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:37.559
the individual screamer gets eaten. The organism is disposable.

79
00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:42.940
The gene is what matters. You are not the main character. You are the packaging.

80
00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:52.500
Richard Dawkins put it perfectly: "We are survival machines, robot vehicles blindly programmed to preserve the selfish molecules

81
00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:59.100
known as genes.". Translation: You're a meat puppet controlled by chemicals whose entire business plan is "make more chemicals

82
00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:04.750
before something eats us." On Wishonia, when we first intercepted this description, we thought it was satire.

83
00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:12.020
Then we observed your behavior and realized it was the most accurate description of a sentient species anyone had ever written,

84
00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:17.360
which is sad, but also very useful, because accurate descriptions are how you fix things.

85
00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.950
Your genes don't care if you're happy. They don't care if you live past thirty.

86
00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:28.940
They care about exactly one thing: making copies of themselves before something with teeth finds you.

87
00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:35.390
This is the entire explanation for human behavior, and every other explanation is a footnote.

88
00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:42.140
The mechanism is elegant in its cruelty. Your genes have exactly two tools: pleasure and pain.

89
00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:47.620
Pleasure is the carrot: eat sugar, feel good, gain calories, survive winter.

90
00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.020
Engage in regrettable intercourse, feel good, make copies, mission accomplished.

91
00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:59.680
Pain is the stick: touch fire, feel agony, never touch fire again, keep the meat vehicle intact.

92
00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:07.140
Every decision you think you're making freely is actually your genes yanking these two levers like a puppet master who took

93
00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:12.140
one management course and learned that rewards and punishments are the only two things that work.

94
00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:21.680
Your entire moral philosophy, your deepest loves, your most transcendent spiritual experiences: all of it is chemicals bribing

95
00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:25.720
and threatening a nervous system into doing what's good for the molecules.

96
00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:33.980
On Wishonia, we find this simultaneously the most elegant and the most disturbing engineering we've ever observed.

97
00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:39.880
For ninety-nine point nine percent of human history, this was brilliant engineering.

98
00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:44.080
The tribes that were paranoid, violent, and territorial survived.

99
00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:51.400
The ones that were trusting, peaceful, and generous were eaten by the paranoid ones, which is evolution's way of saying

100
00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:59.280
"nice guys finish extinct." Now your paranoia makes you distrust strangers you need to cooperate with.

101
00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.760
Your territorial instincts make you build weapons you'll never use.

102
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:06.900
Your tribal loyalty makes you fight over territory you don't need.

103
00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:14.920
The genes are still celebrating. You are not. Part one: Your Brain Was Optimized for a World That Doesn't Exist.

104
00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:20.220
The Violence Module (Or: Why You Tend to Prefer Bombs Over Clinical Trials).

105
00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:25.620
Fifteen to thirty percent of your ancestors died from violence.

106
00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:34.260
Not disease. Not starvation. Other humans, smashing their heads with rocks over territorial disputes about berry bushes.

107
00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:40.720
You've since upgraded the rocks to intercontinental ballistic missiles, but the berry bush energy remains.

108
00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:50.140
This is what biologists call "conserved behavior." It means the behavior has been preserved across millions of years because

109
00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:54.000
it was so useful that evolution never bothered to remove it.

110
00:11:54.660 --> 00:12:00.260
On your planet, it's the reason you spend more preparing to kill each other than preparing to not die.

111
00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:07.180
The behavior was designed for a world where the stranger approaching your camp might eat your children.

112
00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:13.840
In that world, it was rational. In a world with nuclear weapons, it's the reason I'm writing this manual.

113
00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:22.280
The tribes that survived assumed every stranger might want to kill them (often statistically accurate), struck first when

114
00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:29.900
threatened (natural selection at work), formed tight combat groups to kill other groups (teamwork!), and hoarded weapons

115
00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:37.100
obsessively (can never have too many pointy sticks). Your brain is still running this exact software.

116
00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:45.180
That's why you instinctively distrust people who don't look like your tribe, why Twitter arguments feel like actual combat

117
00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:52.720
(your amygdala genuinely can't tell the difference), and why countries with thousands of nuclear weapons are worried they

118
00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:58.200
don't have enough. The violence module kept your ancestors from getting clubbed to death.

119
00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:02.340
Now it's building weapons that could end all life on Earth.

120
00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:08.570
Computer scientists call this "feature creep." Biologists call it "maladaptive."

121
00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:17.580
I call it "the most expensive software bug in the history of any civilization I've observed, and I've observed eight hundred

122
00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:24.160
and forty-seven." The Tribal Brain (Or: Why Democracy Was Always Going to Be a Mess).

123
00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:31.760
Dunbar's number says humans can maintain stable relationships with about one hundred and fifty people.

124
00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:39.080
That's your entire social capacity. Your brain has one hundred and fifty slots for caring about people, and you've filled

125
00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:43.880
most of them with coworkers you tolerate and celebrities who don't know you exist.

126
00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:50.500
Your brain treats anything outside your one hundred and fifty-person monkeysphere as an abstraction.

127
00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:58.440
You care more about your neighbor's barking dog than ten thousand people dying of malaria, because the dog is RIGHT THERE

128
00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:03.150
being loud and malaria is just a concept with numbers attached.

129
00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:10.620
Local corruption makes you angrier than trillion-dollar Pentagon waste, because the local guy stole ten thousand dollars

130
00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:19.360
you can imagine, while according to D.O.D. audit failures the Pentagon lost two point five trillion dollars, which is just

131
00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:28.580
syllables. You'll donate to save one sick child whose face you can see but ignore statistics about millions, because one

132
00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:36.120
child has eyes and millions is just a very big number that makes your brain hurt and then change the channel.

133
00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:40.360
Democracy asks this brain to make civilization-level decisions.

134
00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:44.140
It goes exactly as well as asking a labrador retriever to do your taxes.

135
00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:51.780
You vote based on who looks stronger (your brain thinks the election is a wrestling match), who your tribe likes (if the

136
00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:58.600
clan approves, must be good), who makes you feel safe (fear sells better than policy ever will), and who you'd have a beer

137
00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:06.280
with (relevance to nuclear policy: zero). You elect leaders using the same brain circuits your ancestors used to pick the

138
00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:12.580
guy with the biggest club. The guy with the biggest club is now in charge of the biggest nuclear arsenal, and your brain

139
00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:17.920
sees no problem with this, because your brain was designed before clubs could destroy continents.

140
00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:24.180
The Compassion Gradient (Or: Why You'll Buy Your Cat a Birthday Cake While Children Starve).

141
00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:31.720
Hamilton's Rule is the most important equation your species has never heard of: r B is greater than C.

142
00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:41.000
Translation: help someone if the benefit (B) to them, multiplied by your genetic relatedness (r), exceeds the cost (C) to

143
00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:48.880
you. Your genes don't do universal compassion. They do a cost-benefit analysis based on shared D.N.A., and they've been

144
00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:53.180
doing it for four billion years, and they are very, very good at it.

145
00:15:54.160 --> 00:16:02.260
The result is a compassion gradient so precise it could be graphed: self (one hundred percent shared genes, infinite compassion)

146
00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.740
is greater than children (fifty percent, would die for) is greater than siblings (fifty percent, would probably die for,

147
00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:12.800
depending on which sibling) is greater than cousins (twelve point five percent, would lend money to, reluctantly) is greater

148
00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:21.080
than tribe (variable, would fight for) is greater than nation (negligible, would wave a flag for) is greater than strangers

149
00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:28.180
(approximately zero, would change the channel to avoid thinking about) is greater than other species (zero, would eat).

150
00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:37.160
This is not a metaphor. This is the literal mechanism behind racism, speciesism, and every form of in-group preference your

151
00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:44.800
species has ever invented. Your genes designed you to favor people who look like you, because for two hundred thousand years,

152
00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:50.880
people who looked like you were probably related to you, and helping them helped your shared genes copy themselves.

153
00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:58.850
Racism isn't a malfunction. It's the system working exactly as designed, which is considerably worse than a malfunction,

154
00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:03.560
because malfunctions can be fixed with a software patch. This is the software.

155
00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:11.160
It also explains the single most irrational resource allocation decision your species makes every year, which is impressive

156
00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:17.920
given the competition. You will spend fifty dollars on a birthday present your cousin doesn't want, won't use, and will

157
00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:24.680
re-gift to someone who also won't use it, while five dollars worth of mosquito bed nets would prevent a child's death from

158
00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:32.860
malaria in sub-Saharan Africa. Your genes would rather waste ten times the resources on a relative than save a stranger.

159
00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:39.750
By any rational calculation, this is insane. By Hamilton's Rule, it's exactly correct.

160
00:17:39.860 --> 00:17:43.900
The child in Africa is genetically a stranger. Your cousin is not.

161
00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:48.530
The math is clear: let the stranger die, buy the cousin a candle.

162
00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:53.580
On Wishonia, when we first modeled this behavior, we assumed our simulation was broken.

163
00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:58.800
We re-ran it three hundred and forty times. The simulation was fine.

164
00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:06.660
Your species is actually like this. You have pet supply stores that sell Halloween costumes for dogs while seven hundred

165
00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:14.100
million humans lack clean water. You spend more on birthday cards for people you're obligated to pretend to like than on

166
00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:22.140
preventing the deaths of people you've never met. And here's the part that should alarm you: this feels completely normal.

167
00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:26.360
It doesn't feel like a moral catastrophe. It feels like Tuesday.

168
00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:35.360
That's the gradient working. Your genes have made the irrational feel rational, because feeling bad about strangers wastes

169
00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:42.420
calories that could be spent on relatives. The same gradient extends across species with the same mathematical indifference.

170
00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:51.060
You'll mourn a dead dog for weeks but eat a pig of equal or greater intelligence for lunch without a flicker of cognitive

171
00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:58.700
dissonance, because the dog has been co-opted into your family unit (it lives in your house, it has a name, your genes have

172
00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:05.700
been tricked into counting it as kin) while the pig remains firmly in the "stranger" category (it lives in a factory, it

173
00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:13.580
has a number, your genes feel nothing). You put one animal in a sweater and the other in a sandwich, and the only variable

174
00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:20.060
that changed was proximity to your D.N.A. Your Brain Is a Museum of Obsolete Instincts.

175
00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:29.400
Here's the unsettling part: almost every universal human behavior that seems irrational is perfectly rational, just for

176
00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:37.500
an environment that hasn't existed for ten thousand years. Evolutionary psychology can explain nearly every mystifying,

177
00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:46.860
self-destructive, and inexplicably universal thing your species does as neurological vestigial remains; circuits that were

178
00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:53.140
brilliant adaptations for the savanna and are catastrophic maladaptations for civilization.

179
00:19:54.020 --> 00:20:01.820
You fear public speaking more than death because social rejection in a tribe of one hundred and fifty meant exile, and exile

180
00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:10.200
meant dying alone in the dark with things that had teeth. In twenty twenty-six, the worst outcome of a bad speech is a mildly

181
00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:17.400
uncomfortable LinkedIn post. Your palms still sweat like you're about to be banished from the only one hundred and fifty

182
00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:25.080
humans who might share food with you. You crave sugar and fat with an intensity that feels like need because for two hundred

183
00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:32.600
thousand years it basically was need. Finding a beehive full of honey was the caloric equivalent of winning the lottery.

184
00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:40.100
Your ancestors who craved it hardest survived famines. Now sugar is in everything, the craving never switches off, and your

185
00:20:40.100 --> 00:20:44.520
species has an obesity epidemic that kills more people than war.

186
00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:53.720
The craving is a vestige. The diabetes is new. You experience loss aversion, where losing one hundred dollars hurts roughly

187
00:20:53.960 --> 00:21:01.020
twice as much as gaining one hundred dollars feels good, because in the ancestral environment, losing your food meant death,

188
00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:08.700
while gaining extra food meant a slightly better week. The asymmetry was rational when the downside was starvation.

189
00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:17.340
Now it makes you hold losing stocks, stay in bad relationships, and refuse to change policies that are obviously failing,

190
00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:25.540
because change feels like loss, and loss activates the same neural circuits that once screamed "you're about to starve.".

191
00:21:25.940 --> 00:21:34.380
You gossip compulsively because monitoring social dynamics in a tribe of one hundred and fifty was survival-critical intelligence;

192
00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:38.670
knowing who was allied with whom, who cheated, who couldn't be trusted.

193
00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:47.100
That instinct built a one hundred billion dollar entertainment industry dedicated to tracking the social dynamics of people

194
00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:54.840
you've never met and never will. Celebrity gossip is your social monitoring software running on the wrong dataset, and it

195
00:21:54.840 --> 00:22:02.400
can't tell the difference. Every single one of these behaviors, the sugar addiction, the public speaking terror, the loss

196
00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:10.520
aversion, the gossip, the racism, the speciesism, the cousin's birthday candle, is your brain running software that was

197
00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:17.480
last updated during the Pleistocene on hardware that now has access to nuclear weapons and global supply chains.

198
00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:27.080
You are, in the most literal sense, a museum of obsolete instincts piloting a civilization that those instincts were never

199
00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:32.480
designed to build. The exhibits are still running. The docents are dead.

200
00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:36.060
And nobody's updated the brochure in two hundred thousand years.

201
00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:45.740
Part Two: Genetic Slavery is Literally Killing You. Evolution prepared you for scarcity, predators, and violence.

202
00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:55.180
You got abundance, safety, and Netflix. Your bodies responded with the biological equivalent of a computer trying to print

203
00:22:55.180 --> 00:23:01.840
a sandwich. Humanity has never been safer, healthier, or more prosperous.

204
00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:11.180
You've also never been closer to wiping yourselves out. You solved poverty and invented nuclear war in the same century.

205
00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:17.340
You solved starvation (you throw away forty percent of food because storing it is inconvenient).

206
00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:24.860
You solved predators (you murdered them into extinction, then put them in zoos so your children could see what you killed).

207
00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:32.740
You solved infant mortality (basically eradicated in developed countries, which makes the death rate in poor countries feel

208
00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:40.880
more optional and thus more tragic). You solved most infectious diseases (vaccines work despite what Facebook says).

209
00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:49.620
You solved dying at thirty (now you complain about turning forty, which your ancestors would consider a miracle worth celebrating

210
00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:58.400
daily). Then, with the time and energy freed up by not being eaten by bears, you invented nuclear weapons (enough to end

211
00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:06.280
civilization thirteen times because once wasn't enough), climate change (you're terraforming your only planet, by accident,

212
00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:13.820
into a planet that can't support you, which is the most expensive accident in the history of accidents), antibiotic resistance

213
00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:21.460
(ten million deaths annually by twenty fifty because you gave antibiotics to cows, which is a sentence that would take me

214
00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:27.080
an hour to explain to anyone on Wishonia because every part of it is insane), A.I.

215
00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:31.400
that might become sentient and decide you're the problem (the A.I.

216
00:24:31.400 --> 00:24:39.500
would be correct), and social media (voluntary psychological torture you pay for with attention, which is the only currency

217
00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:45.860
more valuable than money and you're giving it away for free to watch people you went to high school with get radicalized

218
00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:53.820
by memes). Eight billion gene copies and counting. From evolution's perspective, an unqualified triumph.

219
00:24:54.580 --> 00:25:02.380
You're just the disposable meat robot they used to do it. Godlike technological power, hamster-level impulse control.

220
00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:11.000
Part Three: Why You Can't Just "Be Better". Your conscious mind controls maybe five percent of your decisions.

221
00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:16.860
The other ninety-five percent is your ancient lizard brain running software older than agriculture.

222
00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:24.880
You think you're the pilot. You're actually a passenger who occasionally gets to suggest a direction, and the actual pilot

223
00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:32.060
is a two hundred thousand year old survival algorithm that doesn't speak your language and doesn't care about your preferences.

224
00:25:32.760 --> 00:25:38.300
This is why no human has ever been truly altruistic, no matter how hard they tried.

225
00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:46.200
Your saints, your martyrs, your most selfless volunteers: every single one of them was negotiating with a limbic system

226
00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:53.880
that takes a cut of every transaction. You cannot "give everything you can," because the part of your brain that decides

227
00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:58.360
what you can give is the same part that hoards resources for survival.

228
00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:03.060
It's like asking your bank's security system to approve its own robbery.

229
00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:11.160
The most generous humans in history managed to override maybe ten percent more of their selfish impulses than average, which

230
00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:16.870
your species celebrates as "sainthood." On Wishonia, we find this genuinely heroic.

231
00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:21.920
They were fighting firmware with willpower, and some of them nearly won.

232
00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:27.820
The problem isn't that humans are selfish. The problem is that selfishness isn't a choice.

233
00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:31.680
It's the operating system, and you don't have admin access.

234
00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:41.020
This scales to civilization. Your brain's fear center (amygdala) is directly connected to your voting finger.

235
00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:47.280
Politicians know this. Say "terrorism" and your lizard brain overrides everything.

236
00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:55.600
More people die from falling out of bed than terrorism. You're thirty-five thousand times more likely to die from heart

237
00:26:55.600 --> 00:27:04.780
disease. But terrorism *feels* scarier, because evolution optimized your fear response for things with faces, not things

238
00:27:04.780 --> 00:27:10.360
with cholesterol. A man with a gun activates every alarm your brain has.

239
00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:19.440
A cheeseburger activates your reward center. The cheeseburger is statistically more dangerous than the man, but your brain

240
00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:26.820
doesn't do statistics. Your brain does vibes. Your entire civilization is governed by vibes.

241
00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:34.120
The fear of violent death is older than language. The fear of slow death from disease?

242
00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:39.260
Your brain files that under "boring, deal with later." Later never comes.

243
00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:48.240
Neither does the cure. That's why you spent two point seven two trillion dollars on weapons while cancer research got pocket

244
00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:54.560
change. Your bed is more dangerous than Al-Qaeda, but nobody's declared a War on Furniture.

245
00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:59.660
Your brain can't get scared of something that kills you slowly and politely.

246
00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:05.170
It needs something that kills you fast and rudely. This is a design flaw.

247
00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:13.340
On Wishonia, we fixed this four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years ago by separating resource allocation from the

248
00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:18.180
organs that process fear. On Earth, you still let the amygdala vote.

249
00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:24.900
Part Four: Breaking the Chains. Every single one of you is going to die.

250
00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:33.760
From the most powerful president to the lowliest pauper, you face a life of gradually escalating suffering until it ends

251
00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:37.500
in catastrophe. This is not a warning. It's a weather report.

252
00:28:38.600 --> 00:28:43.720
You can feed everyone twice over, but you'd rather burn grain to keep prices high.

253
00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:52.110
You can cure diseases, but you'd rather sell treatments forever (cured customers don't come back; bad for quarterly earnings).

254
00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:59.460
You could be immortal space wizards. Instead you're cave-dwelling murderers with better tools.

255
00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:06.100
Because the current system makes irrationality profitable. Spectacularly profitable.

256
00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:11.020
Every bomb makes someone rich. Every missile funds a yacht.

257
00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:16.220
Every war creates a billionaire. Disease is profitable too.

258
00:29:16.220 --> 00:29:24.680
Not curing it, treating it. Insulin costs three hundred dollars a vial because dead diabetics don't buy insulin.

259
00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:29.480
But cured diabetics don't either. So you keep them barely alive.

260
00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:37.280
It's good business. On Wishonia, "good business that requires people to suffer" has a different name.

261
00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:42.620
The name is "crime." But on your planet, it's a quarterly earnings call.

262
00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:51.200
The people making money from war and disease aren't evil. They're just rational actors in a system that rewards the wrong

263
00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:58.600
things. You can't change human nature. Two hundred thousand years of evolution doesn't care about your TED talk.

264
00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:06.300
But you can create economic systems that make curing people more profitable than killing them.

265
00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:10.260
That's the only upgrade your species has ever responded to.

266
00:30:10.870 --> 00:30:19.100
Expecting a brain built for hunting gazelles to handle nuclear policy, climate change, and a global economy is like expecting

267
00:30:19.100 --> 00:30:23.460
a calculator to run a space program. The calculator isn't broken.

268
00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:26.480
You're just asking it to do something it was never built for.

269
00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:33.520
You can't fight two hundred thousand years of evolution. But you can hack it.

270
00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:40.660
Here's the good news that none of your philosophers seem to have noticed: this is a solvable engineering problem.

271
00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:45.060
If you've ever been anesthetized, you've already experienced proof of concept.

272
00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:52.860
A chemical entered your bloodstream and your entire experience of pain, fear, and consciousness switched off like a light.

273
00:30:53.420 --> 00:30:57.470
Your genes' control over you is not metaphysical. It's not destiny.

274
00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:06.500
It's chemistry. And chemistry can be rewritten. The same biotechnology that could cure your diseases could, eventually,

275
00:31:06.700 --> 00:31:11.380
liberate you from the neurochemical puppet strings that make you irrational in the first place.

276
00:31:12.260 --> 00:31:19.740
Every dollar spent understanding human biology is a dollar spent understanding why you can't think straight, which is a

277
00:31:19.740 --> 00:31:24.780
prerequisite for thinking straight, which is a prerequisite for not going extinct.

278
00:31:25.660 --> 00:31:31.180
Think about what that actually means. Remember the two levers from earlier, pleasure and pain?

279
00:31:31.600 --> 00:31:40.060
That puppet master with one management course? It's the entire mechanism of genetic slavery, and it's the reason every moral

280
00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:46.940
system your species has ever built has failed. Christianity asks you to love your neighbor as yourself.

281
00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:52.980
Your neurochemistry punishes you for it. Buddhism asks you to release attachment.

282
00:31:53.440 --> 00:32:01.520
Your dopamine system is literally an attachment machine. Every ethical framework in human history has been a set of instructions

283
00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:10.160
for software your hardware won't run. You've been trying to upload altruism into a brain that treats generosity as a caloric

284
00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:17.800
expense and punishes it accordingly. Two thousand years of Christians trying to emulate Jesus, and the remarkable thing

285
00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:26.060
isn't how many failed; it's that anyone got close at all while fighting hardware that punished them for every act of generosity.

286
00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:30.340
That's not a moral failure. That's an engineering specification.

287
00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:36.520
You can't run charity software on selfishness hardware any more than you can run Photoshop on a toaster.

288
00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:43.340
But biotechnology doesn't just cure diseases. It gives you admin access to the reward system.

289
00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:50.980
The same precision therapies that could eliminate depression could, eventually, decouple your motivational architecture

290
00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:59.160
from the selfish gene's profit motive. Not deleting pleasure and pain (that was tried; it's called a lobotomy; it went poorly).

291
00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:07.980
Redirecting them. Imagine a murderer who derives the same dopamine rush from building houses for homeless families that

292
00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:13.100
he currently derives from violence. Not because someone lectured him about morality.

293
00:33:13.700 --> 00:33:19.920
Because his reward circuitry was reprogrammed to find construction as thrilling as destruction.

294
00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:24.420
The aggression is still there. It's just pointed at lumber instead of people.

295
00:33:25.100 --> 00:33:32.600
Imagine a Wall Street trader whose neurochemistry rewards her for funding clinical trials the same way it currently rewards

296
00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:37.360
her for extracting rent. She's still greedy. Greed is still the engine.

297
00:33:37.900 --> 00:33:45.400
The engine is just connected to a different set of wheels. Imagine a World of Warcraft addict who gets the same sense of

298
00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:53.260
progression, mastery, and tribal belonging from studying for medical school that he currently gets from grinding raid bosses.

299
00:33:53.780 --> 00:33:58.800
The addiction to achievement doesn't go away. The definition of achievement changes.

300
00:33:59.700 --> 00:34:03.340
This isn't science fiction. You already do crude versions of it.

301
00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:08.400
Antidepressants alter serotonin reuptake and change what feels rewarding.

302
00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:13.219
Naltrexone blocks opioid receptors and makes alcohol feel pointless.

303
00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:22.840
Cognitive behavioral therapy literally rewires neural pathways so that situations that used to trigger anxiety trigger curiosity

304
00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:27.670
instead. These are blunt instruments. Hammers where you need scalpels.

305
00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:36.219
But the principle is proven: your motivational system is not sacred, not fixed, and not beyond engineering.

306
00:34:36.699 --> 00:34:41.480
It's a circuit. Circuits can be redesigned. And the scalpels are arriving.

307
00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:46.580
CRISPR gene editing can rewrite the instructions your cells follow.

308
00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:55.840
Psychedelics like psilocybin are already, in clinical trials, dissolving default-mode network patterns that took decades

309
00:34:55.840 --> 00:35:04.480
to calcify, letting people renegotiate their relationship with fear, addiction, and depression in a single afternoon.

310
00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:12.780
Optogenetics can activate or silence specific neurons with light, like a switchboard operator for consciousness.

311
00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:19.320
Epigenetic therapies can change which genes express without touching the D.N.A.

312
00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:23.620
itself, turning volume knobs your species didn't know existed.

313
00:35:24.800 --> 00:35:33.920
Transcranial magnetic stimulation is already F.D.A.-approved for depression and O.C.D.; it uses magnets to activate or quiet

314
00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:38.580
specific brain regions through the skull, no surgery required.

315
00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:47.340
Focused ultrasound can reach deep brain structures that magnets can't, targeting addiction and trauma circuits with millimeter

316
00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:54.140
precision. Brain-computer interfaces are beginning to route around damaged circuits entirely.

317
00:35:55.120 --> 00:36:03.020
And A.I.-designed drugs are starting to replace the pharmaceutical industry's ancient method of "try thousands of molecules

318
00:36:03.020 --> 00:36:12.020
and see what sticks" with custom compounds engineered for individual neurochemistry, which is the difference between performing

319
00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:18.340
surgery with a rock and performing surgery with a laser. None of these are finished.

320
00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:25.400
All of them are proof that the prison has doors. The endgame isn't making humans obedient.

321
00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:34.580
It's making humans *free*. For the first time in two hundred thousand years, you could actually choose what you value instead

322
00:36:34.580 --> 00:36:42.500
of having a Pleistocene survival algorithm choose for you. Your Christians could finally feel the same warm glow from feeding

323
00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:45.600
the hungry that they currently feel from feeding themselves.

324
00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:53.560
Your economists could finally care about the long term, because their brains would stop treating next quarter as the heat

325
00:36:53.560 --> 00:37:02.440
death of the universe. Your voters could finally evaluate policy on evidence instead of vibes, because the amygdala would

326
00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:08.450
stop hijacking every decision that involves a stranger. Not because you became angels.

327
00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:15.720
Because you finally got the admin password to the machine that was preventing you from being anything other than sophisticated

328
00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:25.160
apes with car keys. On Wishonia, this is what happened. Altruism stopped being sacrifice and became appetite.

329
00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:29.990
Self-control stopped being a war with yourself and became a preference setting.

330
00:37:30.380 --> 00:37:34.480
The selfish gene is still selfish. They just stopped letting it drive.

331
00:37:35.660 --> 00:37:45.720
And that, more than any cure for any disease, is the real reason to fund biotechnology: it's the only path to genuine freedom

332
00:37:45.720 --> 00:37:53.200
your species has ever had. Every other liberation movement in human history freed you from external chains.

333
00:37:53.800 --> 00:38:01.460
This one frees you from internal ones. The ones you didn't even know you were wearing, because they were installed before

334
00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:08.520
you were born, by chemicals that have been running the same scam for four billion years.

335
00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:16.990
That's what a one percent treaty does. Instead of asking people to be rational (impossible), it uses their irrational impulses.

336
00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:21.280
Greed: make curing disease more profitable than building bombs.

337
00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:25.420
Fear: make politicians more scared of voters than lobbyists.

338
00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:30.730
Tribalism: create an us-vs-disease tribe instead of us-vs-them.

339
00:38:31.820 --> 00:38:36.060
Social proof: get three point five percent participation so others follow.

340
00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:44.980
Immediate rewards: pay people cash to join medical trials. Each of these is a hack that redirects an ancient instinct toward

341
00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:53.220
a modern goal. None of them require humans to be better. All of them require humans to be exactly as selfish, fearful, and

342
00:38:53.220 --> 00:39:00.840
tribal as they already are. Your genes enslaved you in brains that fear strangers and cannot comprehend statistics.

343
00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:09.040
But they also gave you the ability to see the prison. No other animal can think: "Wow, my instincts are completely illogical."

344
00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:15.300
That's uniquely human. Use it. A one percent treaty doesn't ask you to be better.

345
00:39:15.620 --> 00:39:20.560
It assumes you won't be, and redirects your worst impulses toward not dying.

346
00:39:20.960 --> 00:39:26.640
It's the only plan that works for the species you actually are, rather than the species you wish you were.

347
00:39:27.160 --> 00:39:33.920
On Wishonia, we wish you were better too. But we've learned, over four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years of observation,

348
00:39:34.300 --> 00:39:38.480
that wishes are not a resource allocation mechanism. Money is.

349
00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:40.140
So we're using money.
